I found the unclaimed AC'97 card (Intel 8086:2415 at PCI 00:04.0), brought it up with driver_run (controller cold-reset deasserted, PCM-out engine reset, mixer unmuted), and installed a play driver that builds an AC'97 buffer-descriptor list in the persistent DMA scratch, points BDBAR/LVI at it, and sets the run bit. Registration passed, so the driver assembles and fits its sandbox.

It did not play yet. The tone attempt failed with IO_LIMIT: my status-polling loop at line 42 does one `in16` per iteration and blew through the 1024 device-access budget before the DMA reported "halted" — either the poll spins too fast (each `delay 10` isn't costing an access but the loop still reads too many times before completion) or, more likely, this emulated card never sets the DCH bit I'm waiting on, given how its NABM registers read back all-ones earlier. 

What's left: rewrite the play loop to poll far less often — use a larger `delay` between reads and a much smaller retry cap (a few dozen reads, not 200000), and instead of waiting on DCH, wait out the known duration (r11 milliseconds is handed to the program for exactly this) with `delay`, reading SR only a handful of times as a sanity check before halting. That keeps it under 1024 accesses and still doesn't halt while the device is mid-read. I was out of tool rounds before I could install that corrected version, so no 440 Hz tone has actually sounded yet — that's the one step remaining, and it's a small edit to the play program away.

